<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wenceslaus II of Bohemia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1271">1271</a>–<a href="/years/1305">1305</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Wenceslaus II Přemyslid · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/premyslid-dynasty">Přemyslid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wenceslaus II was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-of-bohemia">King of Bohemia</a> from 1278 and King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> from 1300. Enriched by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutna-hora">Kutná Hora</a>, where he introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> groschen, he extended Bohemian overlordship over the Silesian duchies and won Greater Poland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, uniting Poland under his rule until his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-ii-of-bohemia.jpg" alt="Wenceslaus II of Bohemia" width="320" height="516" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Master of the Codex Manesse (Additional Painter I) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vaclav2_trun.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The strikes at Kutná Hora made Bohemia one of the largest producers of silver in Europe, and around 1300 <b>Wenceslaus II</b> introduced the Prague groschen, a heavy silver coin minted at Kutná Hora that circulated across Central Europe, and issued a mining code, the Ius regale montanorum.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…succession, including Jean Bodin in his Six Books of the Commonwealth (1576), argued that elections bred faction and civil war, while Polish nobles valued election because each candidate had to promise to respect their privileges, as <b>Wenceslaus II of Bohemia</b> did in the first such undertaking in 1291.</blockquote><a href="/government/elective-monarchy">Elective monarchy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1289 he paid homage to <b>Wenceslaus II of Bohemia</b>, the first Silesian duke to accept Bohemian overlordship.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bytom">Duchy of Bytom</a></li><li><blockquote>Przemysl II was murdered in 1296, and the duchy passed to Wladyslaw I the Elbow-high, <b>Wenceslaus II of Bohemia</b> and Henry of Glogow.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-greater-poland">Duchy of Greater Poland</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Silesian Henry IV Probus and the Bohemian king <b>Wenceslaus II</b>, who ruled from 1291, Wladyslaw I the Elbow-high took Krakow in 1306.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-krakow">Duchy of Krakow</a></li></ul></section>
